{"content":"The bug was in `_ComplementSet.__lt__` in boltons/setutils.py: the guard condition was inverted (`if not (inc is NotImplemented): return NotImplemented`), which caused it to return `NotImplemented` whenever a comparison was actually possible, and to use undefined `inc`/`exc` and run into bogus `_norm_args_notimplemented`-driven NotImplemented handling otherwise — Python then fell back to the reflected `set.__gt__`, which doesn't know about `_ComplementSet`, producing the reported `TypeError: '<' not supported between instances of '_ComplementSet' and 'set'`.

Fixed by changing the condition to `if inc is NotImplemented: return NotImplemented`, matching the pattern already used correctly in `__le__`, `__ge__`, and `__gt__`.

Verified with a manual script exercising `<` in all four combinations (ComplementSet vs set, set vs ComplementSet, ComplementSet vs ComplementSet, reflected) plus sanity checks on `<=`, `>=`, `>` — all now return correct boolean results instead of raising TypeError. Cleaning up the temporary check script now.","tool_calls":[{"id":"cli_8_1","name":"run","arguments":{"command":"rm check_complement.py"}},{"id":"cli_8_2","name":"read_file","arguments":{"path":"boltons/setutils.py","start_line":854,"end_line":867}}]}

